tion in recent years  especially among contact sports athletes  in professional leagues  this is a neurodegenerative disease  which is irreversible  however for today i'll be focusing on  the first two of these sub concussion  and concussion injury  because these often present the greatest  diagnostic challenges  in addition the hope is that early  diagnosis  may prevent later development of more  severe cases of tbi  next i'd like to briefly highlight the  growing concern  of increased tbi cases the u.s  centers for disease control and  prevention  has reported roughly 2.88 million  cases of tbi  last in 2014 and this is up from 153  million  cases back in 2006  representing a 53 percent increase  in tbi related cases these are those  presenting to emergency department  visits hospitalizations  or resulting in deaths  the most common cases of tbi include  sports injury  combat injury to veterans and military  personnel  accidental falls and motor vehicle  crashes in fact it's been reported that  50 percent of tbi cases were due to  motor vehicle crashes  in a recent paper and jama neurology  nonetheless tbi definitions remain the  same regardless  of the underlying cause  before addressing biomarker testing it  will be helpful to understand  signs symptoms and functional tests  associated with concussion injury  common sides and symptoms include  suggestive  symptoms such as headache or nausea  physical symptoms such as  loss of consciousness loss of memory  vomiting  or neurological conditions such as  behavioral  or emotional changes cognitive  impairment or  sleep disturbances  functional testing or non-biomarker  testing  include neuroimaging cranial  computed tomography or cct scanning  is used to visualize fractures or  lesions in the brain  post injury it is considered to be the  gold standard test  and that to which we'll be comparing  some of our biomarker tests  and magnetic resonance imaging or mri is  also used as one of these  modalities  functional scoring algorithms such as  the glasgow coma scale  gcs or the scat 5 the sports concussion  assessment tool  and balance testing or balance error  scoring system  are also used as functional tests for  assessment of  tbi  the glasgow coma scale is a point system  based on assessment of three criteria  eye response verbal response and motor  response  scores range from 3 to 15 with the  following categories  severe head injury is defined as a gcs  score of 8 or less  moderate head injury with a score of 9  to 12  and finally mild head injury or mtbi  with a gcs score of 13 to 15.  so a person may have a perfect score on  the glasgow coma scale rating but still  have  mild concussion injury  scad 5 or the 5th edition of the sports  concussion assessment tool published in  the british journal of sports medicine  has been also used to evaluate tbi  and this is embraced by several sports  organizations  around the world  it relies on a multi-step approach which  includes the gts score as we just saw  with the i  verbal and motor responses plus certain  red flag  items such as serious neurological or  physiological signs and symptoms  other observable signs such as the  person being knocked out  or motionless facial injury after trauma  and finally cognitive testing memory  assessment questions as well as balance  testing  are integrated into the scat 5 scoring  system  with that introduction in mind i'd like  to now focus attention  in part two on the clinical biomarkers  of acute concussion injury  highlighted here are going to be the  role of the blood-based biomarkers  clinical biomarker tests have been used  to detect multiple causes  of underlying acute brain injury  some clinical examples include  diagnosing brain injury upon cns  infections both  viral and bacterial acute ischemic  stroke  and finally concussion injury or tbi  several different body fluid sample  types have been used during the course  of both clinical  studies as well as diagnostic studies  these include  saliva which is non-invasive but  currently limited in applications  it has been used in molecular profiling  such as  micro rna patterns associated with head  injury  cerebrospinal fluid or csf is the most  direct measure of brain derived proteins  within the central nervous system  however it's a very invasive sample to  collect  finally blood samples or more commonly  serum or plasma  are the most popular sample types to use  for these biomarker test assays  it is a less invasive collection  compared to csf  and as an indirect measure serum can  provide detectable changes  of central nervous system derived  proteins  which qualities make for a good  candidate  tbi biomarker in blood  well in general these are going to be  produced by cells within the brain  region  they'll appear in the peripheral blood  upon head injury usually through  damage to the neuronal cells  and also they need to have a good  detection time window  so these increases will be detectable  soon after injury  and also the elevations will remain  above baseline for several hours to few  days after  injury  thus for these biomarkers the biological  functions  are less critical than the physical  properties of the markers we're going to  be looking at  as such the focus in this presentation  is on commonly studied biomarkers  before we look at those biomarkers  we'll look at one mechanism injury and  release of substances  using proteins into the blood after  concussion injury  often through disruption of the  blood-brain barrier  the blood-brain barrier is formed by  brain endothelial cells  connected by tight junctions  trauma to the head may breach the  protective layer  of the blood-brain barrier  and substances normally produced by  neurons astrocyte cells  glial cells may be released into serum  following head injury  and these are the biomarkers of interest  to us  and once in the peripheral blood these  proteins will transiently spike  in concentration until undergoing normal  elimination  usually through renal mechanisms  for this talk i've included six of the  most popular  serum protein biomarkers used in the  field however in no particular order  this includes markers with approved  clinical use  as well as common research use only  markers  while all of them are immunoassay format  the designs  do differ across manufacturers in terms  of detection  and the way that the system is run some  of these are manual tests  whereas others have become more  automated  the first couple that we'll look at  recently  received fda approval in the united  states for  in-vitro diagnostic use or ibd use  these are uchl1 the ubiquitin  c terminal hydrolase l1 and gfab  gliofibrillary acidic protein  other markers shown here have received  diagnostic approval outside of the  united states  such as s 100 beta the calcium binding  protein s 100  beta iso form has been approved in the  european union  for clinical use both for potential  brain injury or tbi assessment  as well as a role in non-tbi cases such  as malignant melanoma  however in the united states this is  labeled as a research use only  test assay neuron specific enolase or  nse  is also a popular biomarker  this enzyme is usually used to detect  neuroendocrine tumors such as a  neuroblastoma but it's also had  potential clinical use  in tbi cases and concussion injury  myelin basic protein is another popular  protein that is studied as a potential  biomarker for tbi  however the major focus of this is using  csf testing rather than blood testing  and in diagnosing multiple sclerosis  or ms and it's actually received u.s  approval as a lab develop test for  clinical diagnostics for  ms patients and finally  prostaglandin d synthase or beta trace  protein  this has also been a popular marker in  research use only  of tbi clinically it's been used to  detect  csf leakage into the nasal cavity or ear  canal  highlighted in this slide are the  diagnostic performance characteristics  of three in vitro diagnostic approved  biomarkers gfap and uchl1 from  banyan biomarkers out of san diego  california  and the s-100 beta test assay from roche  diagnostics  in europe headquartered in manheim  germany  as we can see from these performance  values  we note that the clinical sensitivity  shown also with the 95 confidence  interval around those estimates  are nearly 100 or perfect for all of  these biomarkers  i should note that the tests gfap and  uchl1 are actually interpreted together  so  these values are identical in both the  first two rows  the s100 beta assay from  roche has also shown to have high  clinical sensitivity  or true positive rate of 98.8 percent  on the other hand the clinical  specificity or true negative rate  is quite low only in the mid 30 percent  range  this is coupled to the negative  predictive value and the positive  predictive value  of negative and positive test results  respectively  notice that the tests with the high  clinical sensitivity  also have very strong negative  predictive values  meaning that a negative test will help  to rule out the diagnosis of tbi  nearly 100 percent in all cases  on the other hand the positive  predictive value meaning  the post-test probability after a  positive  biomarker result is fairly equivocal  because we only have a 9.2 percent  post-test probability  with the first two markers in an eleven  percent post-test probability  with the s-100 so a positive result from  these tests  is not very useful and helpful  clinically  and as we'll see it usually means that  the biomarker would need to have a  follow-up with a ct scan  for diagnosis and i've summarized that  in the table shown here again with the  high clinical sensitivity  high negative predictive value as being  good attributes of these biomarkers  whereas the low  clinical specificity and relatively low  positive predictive value  are not very helpful  so their tests are going to be good at  ruling out the need for ct scan  which can benefit the patient because it  reduces costs in the healthcare system  and also can prevent unneeded radiation  exposure that the patient would need to  undergo  in a publication by papa eddal published  in jama neurology  the authors looked at the temporal  profile of the gfap  and uchl1 biomarkers  they used the banyan biomarker tests  the bti test assay which eventually led  to its ibd  approval from this study  but what we note here looking at the  upper cutoff values that they had  established  in nanograms per ml units shown on the y  axis here  gfap has a very low cutoff upper cutoff  value  and uchl1 also has a  relatively low cutoff value compared to  the spikes and rises that we see in both  of these biomarkers  and notice that there's a sharp rise in  serum levels  in both markers after injury  which return to roughly normal or  baseline levels  after 72 hours so they're rapidly  cleared  by the body usually by renal mechanisms  after they've spiked in this transit  manner so there's a very narrow window  in which we can actually use these  markers to detect  acute concussion injury  in a pilot study colleagues and i had  looked at two serum biomarker tests  the s-100 beta as well as the neuron  specific enolase tests  and a study group of amateur mixed  martial arts fighters  we published information on the study a  few years ago  as an abstract and i have the citation  here for those  interested in reading more about the  study design  of most interests to us were changes at  baseline or pre-fight  to post-fight levels to see the time  course of injury  in a small subset of these individuals  we had also  investigated the temporal changes at  four time points  for both of these markers  specifically we're looking at  pre-fight values labeled as a  these were blood draws taken before  one of the mixed martial arts fights in  time point b  this was 30 minutes after the fight had  concluded  within 30 minutes and then we were able  to resample these same individuals  72 hours later and then one week after  the fight  for this small group of 11 subjects we  noted  in the trend increase in the post fight  within 30 minutes after the fight in  both markers  with return at or near baseline by 72  hours for time point c  and to put these values in perspective  i've shown the clinical cutoffs  recommended by the test manufacturer for  both  s100 beta as well as the neuron specific  enolase  so this final part of the talk we'll  explore the  sample serial sampling strategy with  special attention given to  biological variation and reference  change value  given the limitations of diagnostic test  performance  we've already observed with the high  false positive rates  as previously described meaning the low  clinical specificity  there is hope that a serial sampling  strategy can offer an alternative way to  interpret those test results  to highlight a few selected applications  already in diagnostic use for the serial  sampling strategy  the first is acute myocardial infarction  or  ami detection cardiac troponin test  measured in blood  or serum on a suspected patient  having a heart attack we'll follow a  time course  usually at baseline three hours post  admission  and then six hours post admission to  look for the trend and changes  this is very helpful as a rule in and  rule out strategy for someone with  suspected ami  whether that patient needs to be  admitted to the hospital to the icu for  example  another use of the serial sampling  strategy is to detect  acute kidney injury or aki  this is indicated when the baseline  serum creatinine  value in the patient increases by  0.3 milligram per deciliter or 26.5  micromoles per liter  within the previous 48 hours so we're  looking at  a time course of these markers as the as  they change  and finally the use of the reference  change value or rcv  this aids in post-analytical test  interpretation  and relies on having biological  variation estimates  of the clinical test marker to calculate  the rcv  biological variation these data are  descriptive measures of dispersion  biological variation describes a random  fluctuation of biomarkers  due to normal physiological processes  and this fluctuation occurs around the  subject's mean or average test  concentration value  and it's been termed the homeostatic set  point  at that average test concentration value  measures of dispersion that are used to  describe these data  are variance standard deviation  and percent coefficient of variation or  relative standard deviation  those variance components are separated  out using the analysis of variance or  anova test  and biological variation databases  include information for  a multitude of clinical biomarkers  i've included the links to both of these  databases the first from 2014  on the westgard qc site these are  provided in a table format  and the european federation of  laboratory medicine and clinical  chemistry  has a more updated database from  estimate said in 2019 and these are  searchable by test marker  as well  illustrated in this figure are the two  components of biological variation  i've shown this with an example of the s  100 protein  and the concentration on the y axis in  picograms per  ml the observed variation  of the test result between subjects  is referred to as the cvg the  coefficient of variation  of the between subjects or the group  variants  and the within subject variance  or cvi shown here  so for each one of these subjects that  range will be  the cvi in these 12 subjects  we determine the cvg and cvi estimates  from repeat sampling of healthy  individuals and in this case we're  speaking of non-concussed individuals  and this figure represents eight  separate measurements  from 12 subjects with a plot of the  eight individual values shown in the  gray dots  and the mean value for each  individual shown with the blue dots  and note that all these values are  within the interpretive  range much less than 105 for the upper  cutoff  and the median values were in fact 48  picograms per ml  oh and a subset of subjects having  repeat blood draws  in our study my colleagues and i had  looked at the normal baseline  or pre-fight time point concentrations  in order to calculate  and estimate the within person  biological variation the cvi  for both the s-100 and nse biomarkers  further the rcv or reference change  value calculation  shown in the table was used to calculate  that significant change  cutoff value  of note the rcv provides individual  diagnostic assessment in place of  population based reference interval  cutoffs  so instead of using the 105 cutoff that  i just mentioned for s100  here we're going to be using a  percentage change or  rcv to determine significance  and from those calculations we can see  that a one-sided rcv  gives a significant increase of  44.1 percent or greater for s 100 beta  and 54.1 percent or greater for the nse  protein  in order to determine percentage change  we apply the formula shown here which is  the patient's current result  minus their baseline result divided by  the baseline results  then we ask what is the probability that  the percentage change in the test value  from baseline is significant this can be  determined by comparing to the 95  percent  significance rcv value based on  biological  variation data  the benefit of the rcv is it may allow  for increased detection of positive  results  from 87 paired samples baseline  to post fight we compared the clinical  cut-off values  using the population reference interval  established by the manufacturer  in panel a and compare that to the  percent change significance  based on rcv in panel b  notice for the nse test the percentage  of individuals who went from a pre-fight  negative value within the reference  interval to a post-fight  positive value was 27.6 percent  of the fighters however  when we compare that to the significant  rcv  values we observed that 49.4 percent of  the fighters  now had positive changes and the same  thing occurred with the s 100 protein 54  were flagged as positive based on the  reference interval alone  whereas using the rcv calculation  we observed 86.2 percent who were  positive  so again this highlights the fact that  the use of the reference change value  can allow for better detection and  interpretation of positive test results  one of the limitations currently of the  biomarker tests  that we have in use  i'll finish with a few concluding  remarks based on what we have looked at  in this presentation today  first correctly identifying acute mild  traumatic brain injury  remains a continuous clinical challenge  there are diagnostic challenges of the  observational and functional tests  many of these rely on clinicians  subjective assessment  such as the glasgow coma score reading  ct scans often are negative in mild  concussion injury  serum biomarker testing offers  additional tools for the clinician  diversity of brain derived protein  biomarkers have gained increased  attention in the field  these are released into the peripheral  blood after damage to neuronal cells and  it's usually a  transient increase went back to baseline  in roughly 72 hours as we have observed  and current biomarker tests exhibit low  false negative rates or high clinical  sensitivity  but unfortunately suffer from poor  clinical specificity  therefore the serial sampling strategy  may allow for improved diagnostic  accuracy  that strategy compared the result change  between the last two tests  that the patient had to interpret as  positive or negative  and this is based on comparing to the 95  percent  significance level or reference change  value based on biological variation  estimates  for the test  thank you for attending and listening to  this presentation  on clinical utility of concussion injury  biomarkers  if i can help answer any questions don't  hesitate to contact me  at my email address provided here

