ividuals  72 hours later and then one week after  the fight  for this small group of 11 subjects we  noted  in the trend increase in the post fight  within 30 minutes after the fight in  both markers  with return at or near baseline by 72  hours for time point c  and to put these values in perspective  i've shown the clinical cutoffs  recommended by the test manufacturer for  both  s100 beta as well as the neuron specific  enolase  so this final part of the talk we'll  explore the  sample serial sampling strategy with  special attention given to  biological variation and reference  change value  given the limitations of diagnostic test  performance  we've already observed with the high  false positive rates  as previously described meaning the low  clinical specificity  there is hope that a serial sampling  strategy can offer an alternative way to  interpret those test results  to highlight a few selected applications  already in diagnostic use for the serial  sampling strategy  the first is acute myocardial infarction  or  ami detection cardiac troponin test  measured in blood  or serum on a suspected patient  having a heart attack we'll follow a  time course  usually at baseline three hours post  admission  and then six hours post admission to  look for the trend and changes  this is very helpful as a rule in and  rule out strategy for someone with  suspected ami  whether that patient needs to be  admitted to the hospital to the icu for  example  another use of the serial sampling  strategy is to detect  acute kidney injury or aki  this is indicated when the baseline  serum creatinine  value in the patient increases by  0.3 milligram per deciliter or 26.5  micromoles per liter  within the previous 48 hours so we're  looking at  a time course of these markers as the as  they change  and finally the use of the reference  change value or rcv  this aids in post-analytical test  interpretation  and relies on having biological  variation estimates  of the clinical test marker to calculate  the rcv  biological variation these data are  descriptive measures of dispersion  biological variation describes a random  fluctuation of biomarkers  due to normal physiological processes  and this fluctuation occurs around the  subject's mean or average test  concentration value  and it's been termed the homeostatic set  point  at that average test concentration value  measures of dispersion that are used to  describe these data  are variance standard deviation  and percent coefficient of variation or  relative standard deviation  those variance components are separated  out using the analysis of variance or  anova test  and biological variation databases  include information for  a multitude of clinical biomarkers  i've included the links to both of these  databases the first from 2014  on the westgard qc site these are  provided in a table format  and the european federation of  laboratory medicine and clinical  chemistry  has a more updated database from  estimate said in 2019 and these are  searchable by test marker  as well  illustrated in this figure are the two  components of biological variation  i've shown this with an example of the s  100 protein  and the concentration on the y axis in  picograms per  ml the observed variation  of the test result between subjects  is referred to as the cvg the  coefficient of variation  of the between subjects or the group  variants  and the within subject variance  or cvi shown here  so for each one of these subjects that  range will be  the cvi in these 12 subjects  we determine the cvg and cvi estimates  from repeat sampling of healthy  individuals and in this case we're  speaking of non-concussed individuals  and this figure represents eight  separate measurements  from 12 subjects with a plot of the  eight individual values shown in the  gray dots  and the mean value for each  individual shown with the blue dots  and note that all these values are  within the interpretive  range much less than 105 for the upper  cutoff  and the median values were in fact 48  picograms per ml  oh and a subset of subjects having  repeat blood draws  in our study my colleagues and i had  looked at the normal baseline  or pre-fight time point concentrations  in order to calculate  and estimate the within person  biological variation the cvi  for both the s-100 and nse biomarkers  further the rcv or reference change  value calculation  shown in the table was used to calculate  that significant change  cutoff value  of note the rcv provides individual  diagnostic assessment in place of  population based reference interval  cutoffs  so instead of using the 105 cutoff that  i just mentioned for s100  here we're going to be using a  percentage change or  rcv to determine significance  and from those calculations we can see  that a one-sided rcv  gives a significant increase of  44.1 percent or greater for s 100 beta  and 54.1 percent or greater for the nse  protein  in order to determine percentage change  we apply the formula shown here which is  the patient's current result  minus their baseline result divided by  the baseline results  then we ask what is the probability that  the percentage change in the test value  from baseline is significant this can be  determined by comparing to the 95  percent  significance rcv value based on  biological  variation data  the benefit of the rcv is it may allow  for increased detection of positive  results  from 87 paired samples baseline  to post fight we compared the clinical  cut-off values  using the population reference interval  established by the manufacturer  in panel a and compare that to the  percent change significance  based on rcv in panel b  notice for the nse test the percentage  of individuals who went from a pre-fight  negative value within the reference  interval to a post-fight  positive value was 27.6 percent  of the fighters however  when we compare that to the significant  rcv  values we observed that 49.4 percent of  the fighters  now had positive changes and the same  thing occurred with the s 100 protein 54  were flagged as positive based on the  reference interval alone  whereas using the rcv calculation  we observed 86.2 percent who were  positive  so again this highlights the fact that  the use of the reference change value  can allow for better detection and  interpretation of positive test results  one of the limitations currently of the  biomarker tests  that we have in use  i'll finish with a few concluding  remarks based on what we have looked at  in this presentation today  first correctly identifying acute mild  traumatic brain injury  remains a continuous clinical challenge  there are diagnostic challenges of the  observational and functional tests  many of these rely on clinicians  subjective assessment  such as the glasgow coma score reading  ct scans often are negative in mild  concussion injury  serum biomarker testing offers  additional tools for the clinician  diversity of brain derived protein  biomarkers have gained increased  attention in the field  these are released into the peripheral  blood after damage to neuronal cells and  it's usually a  transient increase went back to baseline  in roughly 72 hours as we have observed  and current biomarker tests exhibit low  false negative rates or high clinical  sensitivity  but unfortunately suffer from poor  clinical specificity  therefore the serial sampling strategy  may allow for improved diagnostic  accuracy  that strategy compared the result change  between the last two tests  that the patient had to interpret as  positive or negative  and this is based on comparing to the 95  percent  significance level or reference change  value based on biological variation  estimates  for the test  thank you for attending and listening to  this presentation  on clinical utility of concussion injury  biomarkers  if i can help answer any questions don't  hesitate to contact me  at my email address provided here
 

